  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Phlebia incarnata, commonly known as Coral-pink Merulius, is a wood-decay fungus characterized by its distinctive coral-pink to salmon-colored fruiting bodies. This species belongs to the Meruliaceae family and plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead hardwood in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Coral-pink coloration",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies display a vibrant coral-pink to salmon color that fades with age, making this the most visually distinctive feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Merulioid hymenium",
      "description": "The underside features an irregular gill-like or wrinkled surface (merulioid) rather than true gills or pores",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, resupinate growth",
      "description": "Forms thin, closely attached crusts on wood that flatten against the substrate rather than forming shelf-like structures",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Phlebia incarnata typically grows on dead hardwood logs and fallen branches in moist forests throughout New England, preferring shaded woodland environments with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate appearance, Coral-pink Merulius is a potent wood-decomposer that breaks down the complex polymers in wood, contributing significantly to nutrient cycling in forest ecosystems.",
